## 1. Get to safety first

**Don't chase the thief.** A phone is never worth getting hurt over. If you've been threatened or hurt, or the theft is happening right now, **call Triple Zero (000)** from someone else's phone or ask a nearby shop for help.

## 2. Lock your phone remotely

Lock your phone as quickly as possible so the thief can't get into your apps.

- **Remote Lock:** go to <a href="https://www.android.com/lock" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">android.com/lock</a> on any device, enter your phone number and follow the steps. This only works if Remote Lock was turned on before the theft.
- **Find Hub:** sign in at <a href="https://www.google.com/android/find" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">google.com/android/find</a> with your Google account to see your phone's location, lock it or make it ring.

> If you can see where your phone is, **don't go there yourself**. Give the location to the police instead.

Your mobile provider can't track or locate your phone for you, so these tools, and the police, are your best options.

## 3. Check your email for the thief's photo

If you had **CrookCatcher** installed, check your email. When someone enters a wrong PIN, pattern or password, CrookCatcher takes a photo with the front camera and emails it to you with the GPS location and an estimated address. Thieves almost always try to unlock a phone, so you may already have their photo and where it happened. Keep these emails for the police and your insurer.

## 4. Call your provider to block your SIM and phone

Call your mobile provider (Telstra, Optus, Vodafone or whoever you're with) and tell them your phone was stolen. Ask them to:

- **Suspend your SIM or eSIM**, so the thief can't use your number, make calls or receive your security codes.
- **Block your phone using its IMEI number.**

Australia has one of the best systems in the world for this. Once your provider blocks the IMEI, the block is shared with **every Australian mobile network**, so the phone won't work with any Australian SIM. It's **free**, and it also protects you from paying for calls a thief makes. If your phone turns up later, your provider can unblock it.

Your IMEI is a unique 15-digit number for your phone. If you don't have it written down, your provider can usually find it for you, or it may be on the original box or receipt.

Note that the block only works in Australia, and it can take a few days to show up in the national system. You can check the status with <a href="https://amta.org.au/consumer-advice/cyber/lost-and-stolen-mobiles/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">AMTA's free IMEI checker</a>.

## 5. Call your bank and secure your money

This is the step people most often forget, and it's often where the real damage happens. A thief who gets into your phone can try to access banking apps, Google Wallet or your email to reset passwords.

- **Call your bank** (the number is on the back of your card) and tell them your phone has been stolen.
- **Freeze your cards** and ask them to suspend mobile banking and payment apps like Google Wallet.
- **Check for transactions** you don't recognise over the next few days.

If you think someone is misusing your accounts or identity, <a href="https://www.idcare.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">IDCARE</a>, Australia's national identity and cyber support service, offers free help.

## 6. Report it to the police

Report the theft even if you think the phone won't be found. You'll need a **police report number** for insurance, and reports help police spot theft hotspots and the people behind them.

- **Call 000** if you're hurt, feel unsafe or the theft is happening right now.
- **Otherwise, call the Police Assistance Line on 131 444**, available 24/7, or report it online through your state or territory police website.

Give them your IMEI number, the time and place of the theft, and any evidence you have, like the photos and location from CrookCatcher's email alerts.

## 7. Change your passwords and warn your contacts

- **Change your Google account password** first, then your email, banking and social media passwords.
- **Sign out of your accounts** on the stolen phone. In your Google account, go to *Security > Your devices* and remove the stolen phone.
- **Warn friends and family.** Thieves sometimes message your contacts pretending to be you, asking for money or security codes.

## 8. Erase your phone only as a last resort

If you're sure you won't get the phone back and you're worried about your data, you can erase it remotely through Find Hub. But **once it's erased, you may no longer be able to locate it**, so only do this after you've tried everything else and given the police the location information they need.

## 9. Claim on your insurance

If your phone is insured, through a phone policy, home and contents insurance or a credit card benefit, contact your insurer with your police report number and IMEI.

---

## How to be ready before it happens

Most of the steps above work much better if you prepare in advance. It takes 10 minutes:

- **Save your IMEI.** Dial `*#06#` and save the number somewhere other than your phone, like your email.
- **Turn on Remote Lock and Find Hub**, so you can lock and locate your phone from any device.
- **Turn on Theft Detection Lock**, which locks your phone automatically if it's snatched from your hand.
- **Hide message previews on your lock screen**, so thieves can't read your security codes.
- **Back up your phone regularly**, so losing it doesn't mean losing your photos and data.
- **Install CrookCatcher**, so you get a photo and the location of anyone who tries to unlock your phone.

For step-by-step instructions, read our guides to the [top 5 Android settings to secure your phone against theft](/blog/top_five_settings) and [how to stop thieves from turning off your phone](/blog/block-power-menu).

